{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Basic Syntax in C#"}, {"type": "position", "content": 4}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear/use when talking about basic syntax in C#."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob04re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Used to and Would"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use <strong>used to </strong>to talk about past habits that we don't do anymore. We use <strong>would</strong> to talk about repeated actions in the past."}, {"type": "text", "content": "</br> Example: <i>I <strong>used to</strong> start programming at 8:00 a. m. everyday</i>.  </br></br>  If we use this structure in the negative form, we use the auxiliary <strong>did not</strong> and the modal <strong>use to</strong> in its base form.  </br></br>  Negative form structure: Subject + <strong>didn't use to</strong> + complement </br>Example: <i>Laura <strong>didn't use to</strong> program with C#.</i></br></br>  As we can see in this example, we are talking about something that didn't happen before but happens now. It allows us to see that we use the <strong>used to</strong> structure to express difference between two recurrent events or actions tied to the path of time.</br></br>  We use <strong>would</strong> for repeated actions in the past. The difference here is that we talk about only actions instead of habits.</br></br>  Structure: Subject + <strong>would</strong> + verb + complement.</br> Example: <i>I <strong>would</strong> write the namespace, define the scope, and start with the classes.</i></br></br>  <strong>Note:</strong> You can't use this structure in the negative or interrogative forms."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I also used to think that, but programming is about practice."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I used to try to remember the theory; I didn't realize that you learn easier when practicing."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Some months ago, I would spend a lot of time to practice programming at home."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "C# Keywords."}, {"type": "describer", "content": "There are some predefined keywords to the C# compiler. You shouldn't use these words as identifiers because it would create conflict in the code. If there's no option but to use one of these words, you use the symbol @ before the word.</br></br>   Some other words have a contextual meaning. It means that they carry certain functions depending on the place they appear on.</br></br>  The following charts have some reserved and some contextual keywords in C#. To look for the whole set of words, you can browse on the internet; some words are often generated and you ought to be up to date with them.</br> <center><strong>Reserved Words</strong>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":13,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" abstract\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"base\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"break\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\